X11 WSI: without TAWC-DRI, present through standard DRI3 and Present.

Termux:X11 has no TAWC-DRI, but imports an Android hardware buffer read from a socket passed as a DRI3 buffer fd
(modifier 1255). Each swapchain buffer is imported once as a pixmap, frames are PresentPixmap requests, and
PresentIdleNotify releases buffers the way TAWC-DRI's buffer-release event does. Its depth-24 windows show any
buffer opaque, so their swapchains can be OPAQUE whatever the format (Zink always asks for it).

Also accept DirectColor visuals, as Mesa's X11 WSI does: GLX gives Zink's windows one, and its pixels are laid out
like TrueColor's.

Mali's driver draws BGRA but can't back a hardware buffer with it, while X11 windows are BGRA and Zink asks for
their format. There a swapchain draws into ordinary BGRA images and copies each frame, byte for byte, into RGBA
buffers: the X server reads them in the window's BGRX order whatever their format says. The copies are recorded once
per image on the presenting queue's family. HYBRIS_SWAPCHAIN_BLIT=1 does the same on any driver, to test it.

HYBRIS_SWAPCHAIN_TEST=1 paints the copied buffers' top half magenta and copies only the bottom half of each frame, to
tell a screen that never shows the buffers from frames that are black.

